Let pending dentry teardown run under Harmony virtual clocks

shrink_dcache_parent can find entries already being destroyed by another
task without collecting any entries of its own. cond_resched only switches
when need_resched is set. On Harmony's single-vCPU guest, an exit-free retry
loop does not advance the virtual clock, so no scheduler tick arrives to
resume the other teardown task. Default HotSpot termination after procfs
thread inspection reproduces this stall in d_walk/select_collect2.

When the first walk finds pending cleanup but no work to dispose, sleep for
one jiffy before retrying/helping. This releases the CPU and lets a timer
advance the deterministic clock if nothing else is runnable. The wait is
outside dentry locks and RCU read-side sections; retain the normal path on
kernels without either Harmony clock. No dentry is skipped or marked freed.

This patch is Linux-kernel code (GPL-2.0), carried under the repository's
kernel-patch exception.

---
--- a/fs/dcache.c
+++ b/fs/dcache.c
@@ -1555,6 +1555,9 @@ void shrink_dcache_parent(struct dentry *parent)
 		cond_resched();
 		if (!data.found)
 			break;
+		if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HARMONY_PVCLOCK) ||
+		    IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_HARMONY_ARM_PVCLOCK))
+			schedule_timeout_uninterruptible(1);
 		data.victim = NULL;
 		d_walk(parent, &data, select_collect2);
 		if (data.victim) {
